WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.130
One man was given a room, a map, and five weeks.

2
00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:09.380
Millions of people did not know which country they would wake up in before they saw the line he drew.

3
00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:15.590
When the work was done he refused his fee, burned his notes, and never set foot on that soil again.

4
00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:34.320
On the evening of the third of June, nineteen forty-seven, when the radio microphones went live in Delhi, a single sentence from the mouth of India's last Viceroy, Louis Mountbatten,

5
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.930
abruptly compressed the calendar of a land where four hundred million people lived.

6
00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:46.420
In February, the British government had announced in Parliament that power would be transferred no later than June nineteen forty-eight.

7
00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:51.990
That left sixteen months, then, to plan the splitting of a country in two.

8
00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:59.480
That evening, Mountbatten erased that timeline and put a new date in its place: the fifteenth of August, nineteen forty-seven.

9
00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.970
The difference between the two was seventy-three days.

10
00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:16.860
In seventy-three days, two states would be founded, an army divided, a treasury split, a railway network parcelled out between two flags — and, hardest of all, something nobody yet possessed would have to be invented:

11
00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.910
a border.

12
00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:23.520
To understand this haste, you have to look at the Punjab and Bengal of that year.

13
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:34.050
Across the rest of the Indian subcontinent, partition was largely a matter of administrative cartography; provinces would be assigned to one side or the other according to their majorities.

14
00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:37.060
But two provinces resisted that logic.

15
00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:47.590
According to the nineteen forty-one census, roughly fifty-three percent of the Punjab's population was Muslim, close to thirty percent Hindu, and about fifteen percent Sikh.

16
00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:52.040
In Bengal, Muslims again held a narrow majority.

17
00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:58.730
In these two provinces the majorities shifted district by district, town by town, even street by street.

18
00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:07.980
Nearly half of Lahore's population was Muslim, yet most of the city's shops, printing houses, and grand residences were in the hands of Hindu and Sikh families.

19
00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:14.350
Amritsar was the holy city of the Sikhs, but the composition of the villages around it was disputed.

20
00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:23.440
Calcutta was the subcontinent's largest port and its industrial heart; almost all of the jute fields that fed it lay in the Muslim-majority districts to the east.

21
00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:33.490
Drawing a line, in other words, meant dividing not only people but canals, railways, power stations, factories, and the fields that supplied them.

22
00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:40.980
Mountbatten, who took office at the end of March, had been repeating one thing from the day he arrived in Delhi: there was no time.

23
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:53.910
The wave of violence that began in Calcutta in August nineteen forty-six had spread to Bihar, to Noakhali, to the United Provinces, and by March nineteen forty-seven had reached the Sikh villages around Rawalpindi.

24
00:02:53.910 --> 00:03:01.080
Mountbatten's calculation was this: the longer partition was delayed, the less there would be of a state left to hand over.

25
00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:09.050
His critics' calculation was precisely the opposite: in a country partitioned this fast, nobody would be able to protect anybody.

26
00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:16.060
History would furnish ammunition to both sides — but on the evening of the third of June, it was clear which side had won.

27
00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:20.990
The Indian Independence Act received royal assent on the eighteenth of July.

28
00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:31.360
The Act created two dominions, and it also set out how the borders were to be determined: two Boundary Commissions would be established, one for the Punjab, one for Bengal.

29
00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:39.650
Each commission would have four high court judges; the Indian National Congress would nominate two, and the All-India Muslim League the other two.

30
00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:47.620
On the Punjab commission, Mehr Chand Mahajan and Teja Singh would sit on one side, Din Muhammad and Muhammad Munir on the other.

31
00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:49.990
Bengal had a similar quartet.

32
00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:59.560
The design itself was a deadlock: two of the four judges would always represent one side and two the other, and on no question would a three-quarters majority ever emerge.

33
00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:06.250
And so each commission was given a chairman who belonged to neither side — and with him, a casting vote.

34
00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:10.380
It is this fine detail that explains everything that followed.

35
00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:18.190
If the four judges could not agree — and that they would not agree was obvious from the start — the decision would rest with a single man.

36
00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:24.560
The pages the four judges signed would amount to little more than appendices recording the positions of the parties.

37
00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:31.890
In practice, the line dividing the Punjab and Bengal would be drawn not by a panel of five, but by a single pen.

38
00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:35.700
The owner of that pen was announced on the thirtieth of June.

39
00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:45.590
In London, agreement had been reached on the one name both sides could accept: a lawyer with no ties to India, no history there, not a single acquaintance.

40
00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:52.600
Congress and the Muslim League alike consented to the choice, because neither side wanted the other side's man.

41
00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:56.570
Ignorance was taken to be the safest measure of impartiality.

42
00:04:56.570 --> 00:05:02.140
The man who stepped off the plane in Delhi in early July would, six weeks later,

43
00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:10.270
determine which state ninety million people would be citizens of — and he would never have spent a single day on the land he had come to divide.

44
00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:17.760
Cyril John Radcliffe was born in eighteen ninety-nine in the north of Wales, educated at Haileybury and at Oxford.

45
00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:27.170
In the final year of the First World War, a problem with his eyes kept him off the front line and sent him instead to the ambulance service; after the war he returned to the law.

46
00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:34.340
He rose quickly at the bar; in nineteen thirty-five, at the age of thirty-six, he was made King's Counsel.

47
00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:39.110
He was one of the most expensive names in tax law and corporate litigation.

48
00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:50.760
During the Second World War he served as Director General of the Ministry of Information — which is to say, he sat at the top of the bureaucracy that managed Britain's wartime propaganda and the flow of its news.

49
00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:58.730
He had a reputation as a cool mind, a man who read files fast and could grasp and summarise a tangled question in a matter of hours.

50
00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:05.420
He was also a man who read literature, loved poetry, and turned Latin phrases over in the evenings.

51
00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:08.590
He had never been trained in cartography.

52
00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:11.440
He had never carried out a land survey.

53
00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:21.970
He did not know the canal system of the Punjab, or how the riverbeds of Bengal shifted from year to year, or what it meant for one village's grazing land to lie inside the boundaries of the next.

54
00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:26.100
And until the age of forty-eight, he had never seen India.

55
00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:31.670
On the eighth of July, nineteen forty-seven, he landed in Delhi.

56
00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:38.200
The city was in the heaviest season of the year, scorched by the humidity that comes before the monsoon.

57
00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:46.490
He was given a government house set in a garden; he worked under the ceiling fans, fell ill, lost his appetite, lost weight.

58
00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:57.500
With him was his private secretary, Christopher Beaumont, a colonial officer who had served for years in the Punjab — it would not be wrong to say he was the only man there who genuinely knew the region.

59
00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:03.230
The terms of reference placed in Radcliffe's hands were, astonishingly, very short.

60
00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:09.920
The commissions were to determine the boundary by identifying the contiguous majority areas of Muslims and non-Muslims.

61
00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:16.930
At the end of the sentence there was an addition: in doing so, he would also take into account other factors.

62
00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:20.580
Those three words were the door to the entire argument.

63
00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:22.630
Which factors?

64
00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:30.600
Rivers, roads, the irrigation of fields, a city's tax revenue, a port's hinterland, a temple held sacred?

65
00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:33.930
The terms of reference did not say.

66
00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:41.580
Radcliffe had been handed the authority to settle everything London could not bring itself to decide — and he had five weeks to do it.

67
00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:44.750
The commissions began work in the middle of July.

68
00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:53.680
The Bengal commission held open hearings in Calcutta, the Punjab commission in Lahore; the Lahore hearings were squeezed into the last ten days of July.

69
00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:57.170
In both cities the halls were packed to the walls.

70
00:07:57.170 --> 00:08:06.100
Lawyers, communal associations, chambers of commerce, farmers' unions, religious institutions and municipalities piled up petitions.

71
00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:16.310
The Congress side wanted the commission to look beyond the census figures, the Muslim League side wanted the census applied to the letter; on some questions the positions were exactly reversed.

72
00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:24.120
Sikh representatives made their own separate case: their community formed a majority in no large district, yet their lands,

73
00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:29.530
their shrines and the most fertile of their farms were scattered right across the middle of the Punjab.

74
00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:34.460
They argued that the boundary should run much further west, along the river Chenab.

75
00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:40.350
There was nothing in the arithmetic of population that could support this demand, and it was refused.

76
00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:45.920
Radcliffe listened to all of it, but he did his real work in Delhi, behind closed doors.

77
00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:49.730
He never went out to the countryside the line would cross.

78
00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:58.180
He never laid eyes on a village that would be split, a canal that would be cut in two, or a farm that within a week would be stranded between two countries.

79
00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:06.310
Years later his secretary would write plainly that his employer had never seen the ground and that his knowledge of the region was close to nothing.

80
00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:09.000
What did Radcliffe himself think?

81
00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:13.770
The clearest trace of it sits in a letter he wrote to his stepson on the eve of partition.

82
00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:22.540
He wrote that nobody in India would love him for the Punjab and Bengal decision, that roughly eighty million people would start looking for him with a grievance.

83
00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:25.870
And he added: I do not want them to find me.

84
00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:29.840
Twenty years later, W.

85
00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:31.250
H.

86
00:09:31.250 --> 00:09:35.700
Auden wrote a short and merciless poem with Radcliffe at its centre.

87
00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:43.830
The idea it opens with is a simple one: because he had never seen the land he was summoned to divide, he was at least impartial.

88
00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:50.520
Auden's mockery was aimed at precisely that — at the treating of impartiality and ignorance as one and the same thing.

89
00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:54.490
What was the raw material on Radcliffe's desk?

90
00:09:54.490 --> 00:10:02.460
Three things: the nineteen forty-one census, the maps printed by the Survey of India, and thousands of pages of petitions.

91
00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:07.230
The nineteen forty-one census was the most fragile part of the whole business.

92
00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:11.680
It had been carried out in the middle of a war, with limited staff.

93
00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:18.530
More importantly, from the end of the nineteen thirties onwards the census in India had turned into a political instrument;

94
00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:28.100
communal organisations ran campaigns urging their members to declare themselves correctly to the enumerators, because everyone knew the numbers meant representation and quotas.

95
00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:38.150
So the tables in his hands were not neutral documents showing the religious composition of a village; they were the remains of a tally taken six years earlier that was itself disputed.

96
00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:40.520
The maps were a separate matter.

97
00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:43.050
Some had been printed decades before.

98
00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:50.540
In Bengal the rivers had shifted their beds, and a branch that flowed from the north on paper had in reality slipped to the south.

99
00:10:50.540 --> 00:11:00.110
In the Punjab, the canals dug in the last twenty years, the towns founded, the railway lines extended, had all made it onto the sheets incompletely.

100
00:11:00.110 --> 00:11:03.120
The line was drawn from this data.

101
00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:07.890
In the Punjab, the first great knot was Lahore and Amritsar.

102
00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:13.780
The two cities lay fifty kilometres apart, and each was a city neither side could bear to give up.

103
00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:18.070
Radcliffe gave Lahore to Pakistan and Amritsar to India.

104
00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:29.560
Years later, at his home in London, the answer he gave to an Indian journalist who asked him about that decision lays the logic of the whole business bare: he said he had very nearly given Lahore to India,

105
00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:37.050
until he realised that Pakistan would then be left without a single major city — because he had already assigned Calcutta to India.

106
00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:44.860
In other words, the reasoning behind a decision in the Punjab was another decision, taken two thousand kilometres away in Bengal.

107
00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:47.550
The second knot was Gurdaspur.

108
00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:56.480
In this district of four subdivisions, Muslims held the slimmest of majorities; on pure arithmetic, it would have been expected to go to Pakistan.

109
00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:05.570
Radcliffe gave the subdivisions east of the Ujh river — Gurdaspur, Batala and Pathankot — to India, and only Shakargarh to Pakistan.

110
00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:16.900
The consequence of that decision was something nobody spoke of aloud in those days, but which would shortly change everything: India now held the only corridor by which it could reach Jammu and Kashmir overland.

111
00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:22.150
When the war over Kashmir began in October, Indian troops used that corridor.

112
00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:30.440
There is no evidence that Radcliffe decided as he did with Kashmir in mind; but there is no doubt whatsoever that his decision made Kashmir possible.

113
00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:35.850
The third knot became the most disputed episode in this whole story: Firozpur.

114
00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:43.500
In Radcliffe's first draft, several subdivisions of this district on the bank of the Sutlej river stood on the Pakistani side.

115
00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:56.590
The draft came up in the second week of August, over a lunch in Delhi at the home of Mountbatten's chief of staff; also at the table were Mountbatten, Jawaharlal Nehru, and the Governor-General's adviser, V.

116
00:12:56.590 --> 00:12:58.480
P.

117
00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:00.370
Menon.

118
00:13:00.370 --> 00:13:07.860
After that lunch Radcliffe altered his draft, and Firozpur, along with two neighbouring subdivisions, passed to India.

119
00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:19.190
After Radcliffe's death, his secretary Beaumont set down a note about that meeting, arguing that the boundary had been redrawn in the final days to Pakistan's detriment, and that Mountbatten had intervened.

120
00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:26.520
Mountbatten's defenders rejected this; because of the gaps in the documentary record, the argument remains unsettled to this day.

121
00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:37.370
What is certain is this: in the closing days of a five-week process, outside any formal session, a detail discussed over a lunch table changed the citizenship of hundreds of thousands of people.

122
00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:40.860
In Bengal the equation was different.

123
00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:51.710
Calcutta went to India; and in order to secure the upper basin of the Hooghly river, the city's lifeline, Murshidabad — with its Muslim majority — was also assigned to India.

124
00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:56.960
In exchange, Khulna, with its Hindu majority, was left to Pakistan.

125
00:13:56.960 --> 00:14:04.770
So two districts changed hands in precisely the opposite direction from what their populations dictated; the reason was the flow of a river.

126
00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:09.540
The fiercest objection came over the Chittagong Hill Tracts.

127
00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:21.670
This region, almost none of whose inhabitants were Muslim, home to hill peoples who were largely Buddhist, was given to East Pakistan on the grounds of its economic ties to the port and to the Karnaphuli river.

128
00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:27.720
The people of the region protested, Nehru objected, and the decision did not change.

129
00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:34.410
And beneath all these great headlines lay the map's real victims, the ones never discussed: the villages.

130
00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:38.060
In places the line ran straight through the middle of a settlement.

131
00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:42.830
A family's house ended up in one state, its field in the other.

132
00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:55.120
The irrigation network of the Punjab was one of the most advanced canal systems in the world, and that network was cut in two; the water of the Upper Bari Doab Canal was drawn from the Madhopur headworks inside India,

133
00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:58.290
and it watered fields inside Pakistan.

134
00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:04.980
A hydrological whole that engineers had built up over decades was split into two pieces under the stroke of a pen.

135
00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:08.310
Radcliffe signed both awards on the twelfth of August.

136
00:15:08.310 --> 00:15:14.040
The sealed envelopes reached the Governor-General's residence a day later, and were placed in a safe.

137
00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:16.410
And then that safe was not opened.

138
00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:21.500
Mountbatten decided that the award must not be announced before the independence ceremonies.

139
00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:28.190
His reasoning was this: if the boundary were made public, the celebrations of the fifteenth of August would drown in blood.

140
00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:38.400
Years later he offered a blunter explanation as well; he said that if the award were proclaimed before independence, the responsibility for enforcing it would have rested on the British.

141
00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:51.490
On the sixteenth of August, in Delhi, he gathered Nehru, Vallabhbhai Patel, Liaquat Ali Khan and Abdur Rab Nishtar for the Muslim League, and Baldev Singh representing the Sikhs, and handed them the envelopes.

142
00:15:51.490 --> 00:15:55.620
The text was made public on the seventeenth of August.

143
00:15:55.620 --> 00:16:03.430
So when Pakistan came into being on the fourteenth of August, and India on the fifteenth, neither country had a border.

144
00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:10.760
Or rather, the border existed — it had been drawn, it had been signed — but it was inside a safe in Delhi.

145
00:16:10.760 --> 00:16:13.450
Those two days are worth dwelling on.

146
00:16:13.450 --> 00:16:24.780
In hundreds of towns across the middle of Punjab and Bengal, mayors, police station commanders, railway stationmasters, teachers and postal clerks did not know which state they were servants of.

147
00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:29.390
In some towns two flags were raised over the main square at once.

148
00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:33.520
In others a single flag went up, and came down two days later.

149
00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:43.090
In Lahore and in Amritsar, people were living under the smoke of fires that had been burning for days, living on rumours about which side of the line their own city would end up on.

150
00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:51.860
The rumours spread faster than the decision itself, and every rumour set off a new wave of flight, a new attack, a new act of revenge.

151
00:16:51.860 --> 00:17:02.390
Not knowing which state a village had fallen to meant being unable to decide whether to stay or to run; and those who could not decide were very often forced to decide at the worst possible moment,

152
00:17:02.390 --> 00:17:04.600
in the worst possible way.

153
00:17:04.600 --> 00:17:11.130
The force expected to keep order was the Punjab Boundary Force, which went into service on the first of August.

154
00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:21.180
Under the command of Major General Thomas Wynford Rees, this combined force had a total strength of around fifty thousand, of which only about half were fighting troops.

155
00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:31.710
It had been assembled out of units of the Indian Army that had not yet been divided along communal lines; which is to say, in the very same company there were men who, within a few weeks,

156
00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:38.080
would be soldiers of two separate countries, and in some units troops were deployed against their own communities.

157
00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:46.210
This force was charged with protecting a region that stretched for hundreds of kilometres from one end to the other and contained tens of thousands of villages.

158
00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:48.420
It was not enough.

159
00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:52.710
The massacres reached their fiercest point in precisely these weeks.

160
00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:59.080
The force was dissolved on the first of September, and responsibility was handed to the two new armies.

161
00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:04.170
Then began the largest movement of human beings the subcontinent has ever seen.

162
00:18:04.170 --> 00:18:09.420
In Punjab alone, close to ten million people were displaced within a few months.

163
00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:20.270
On the roads running east and west, columns formed that stretched for kilometres; ox carts, loaded backs, elderly people who could no longer walk, children born along the way.

164
00:18:20.270 --> 00:18:26.960
Trains filled up in both directions, and some pulled into their destination stations carrying nothing but the dead.

165
00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:35.090
The figures reflected in the nineteen fifty-one census put the number of post-partition refugees at more than fourteen million.

166
00:18:35.090 --> 00:18:44.820
For the number of dead there is no reliable record at all; estimates range from two hundred thousand to two million, and most historians speak of a figure somewhere around one million.

167
00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:51.990
None of these numbers fully accounts for the women who vanished, the children who were abducted, the families who could never return.

168
00:18:51.990 --> 00:19:00.920
The direct cause of all this was not a line; the causes lay in years of politics, incitement, arming, and a collapsing apparatus of state.

169
00:19:00.920 --> 00:19:07.290
But the line being two days late added one more thing on top of those causes: ignorance.

170
00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:11.580
People became independent in a country where they did not know where they belonged.

171
00:19:11.580 --> 00:19:16.350
By the time the award was published, Radcliffe was no longer in Delhi.

172
00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:22.240
The two things he did before he left became the two most talked-about gestures in this whole story.

173
00:19:22.240 --> 00:19:26.370
The first: he burned all of his working papers.

174
00:19:26.370 --> 00:19:33.700
Draft maps, marginal notes, calculations, the reasoning he had written out in his own hand — all of it gone.

175
00:19:33.700 --> 00:19:38.950
What remained were only the signed official texts and the dissenting notes the judges had appended.

176
00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:44.520
The second: he refused to accept the forty thousand rupees he was owed for his services.

177
00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:49.130
There is no detailed explanation in his own words as to why he did this.

178
00:19:49.130 --> 00:19:59.180
And no one ever learned what lay behind those burned notes — which village he had hesitated over, which draft he had changed and why, whose particular sentence had nudged his pencil.

179
00:19:59.180 --> 00:20:02.190
Radcliffe never returned to India.

180
00:20:02.190 --> 00:20:09.520
He went on to a distinguished career in Britain; in nineteen forty-nine he was appointed to the judicial side of the House of Lords,

181
00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:17.170
and in the years that followed he chaired commissions of inquiry on the most sensitive questions facing the British state, and was made a viscount.

182
00:20:17.170 --> 00:20:25.460
When he died in nineteen seventy-seven, he was remembered not for the judgments he handed down as a judge, but for a line he had drawn on a map.

183
00:20:25.460 --> 00:20:35.990
He was conscious of this himself; he is said to have remarked that the award could have been better, that the time he was given made that impossible, and that with more of it he would have done the job differently.

184
00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:43.480
But the real final chapter of this story did not end even with Radcliffe's death, because that line went on living a life of its own.

185
00:20:43.480 --> 00:20:46.810
The crisis that erupted fastest was water.

186
00:20:46.810 --> 00:20:57.820
In April nineteen forty-eight, India shut off the canal water flowing to Pakistan from headworks on its own territory; the crops of Pakistani Punjab were left dry at harvest time.

187
00:20:57.820 --> 00:21:04.030
A provisional agreement signed in May restored the water, but the problem stayed on the table for twelve years.

188
00:21:04.030 --> 00:21:13.920
Finally, in nineteen sixty, with the World Bank acting as mediator, the Indus Waters Treaty was signed in Karachi; the rivers were divided between the two countries.

189
00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:23.650
To solve a single technical problem created by a border drawn in five weeks in nineteen forty-seven, it took the two governments thirteen years and an international arbiter.

190
00:21:23.650 --> 00:21:29.380
That treaty remains, to this day, one of the most strained files between the two countries.

191
00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:33.030
The oddities the line left behind did not end there.

192
00:21:33.030 --> 00:21:45.480
On the Bengal side, hundreds of small parcels of land, inherited from the old princely boundaries, were turned overnight into international enclaves: villages belonging to one country but sitting inside the other,

193
00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:47.690
completely surrounded.

194
00:21:47.690 --> 00:21:56.940
In some of them, people had to cross a foreign country just to reach the territory of their own state; they had no access to schools, to hospitals, to courts.

195
00:21:56.940 --> 00:22:09.230
That problem was resolved in twenty fifteen, through a border agreement ratified between India and Bangladesh; more than one hundred and sixty enclaves were exchanged, and roughly fifty thousand people learned at last,

196
00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:14.320
with certainty, sixty-eight years on, which country they were citizens of.

197
00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:26.290
In the Chittagong Hill Tracts, meanwhile, the tension the line left behind lasted for decades; the region emerged from a long period of armed conflict only with a peace accord signed in nineteen ninety-seven.

198
00:22:26.290 --> 00:22:30.900
On the Punjab side, the line came to symbolise something entirely different.

199
00:22:30.900 --> 00:22:41.750
Kartarpur, where Guru Nanak, the founder of Sikhism, spent the final years of his life, ended up on the Pakistani side of the border, only a few kilometres in from the line.

200
00:22:41.750 --> 00:22:50.360
For decades, pilgrims on the Indian side came to the frontier to look at the white dome of the shrine through binoculars; they could see it, but they could not go.

201
00:22:50.360 --> 00:22:55.770
In November of twenty nineteen, the two countries opened a visa-free walking corridor.

202
00:22:55.770 --> 00:23:02.620
Nankana Sahib, where Guru Nanak was born, also remained on that same side, in Pakistan.

203
00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:12.350
Today the border between India and Bangladesh runs for more than four thousand kilometres, much of it strung with barbed wire; it is one of the longest border fences in the world.

204
00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:19.040
Some stretches of the India–Pakistan border are floodlit brightly enough at night to be seen from space.

205
00:23:19.040 --> 00:23:31.170
All of these installations, these watchtowers, these floodlights follow a line drawn in the summer of nineteen forty-seven by a lawyer sitting under an electric fan in Delhi, a man who had never seen the region,

206
00:23:31.170 --> 00:23:34.500
working from old maps and a disputed census.

207
00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:43.270
Radcliffe himself, I think, offered the sharpest criticism of the outcome: when he said the time he had been given was not enough to do the job properly,

208
00:23:43.270 --> 00:23:48.520
he was in fact describing the real legacy an empire leaves behind as it withdraws.

209
00:23:48.520 --> 00:23:51.690
The line was drawn in five weeks.

210
00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:57.740
But living with it has stretched across eighty years for hundreds of millions of people, and it goes on still.

211
00:23:57.740 --> 00:24:01.390
Nöron History'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

212
00:24:01.390 --> 00:24:05.040
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
